Let's analyze the operation. We want to cover all 'B's with intervals of length $k$. Since the operation turns cells to 'W', the order doesn't strictly matter for the final state, but we want to minimize the number of intervals. This is equivalent to covering all indices $i$ where $s[i] == 'B'$ with the minimum number of intervals of length $k$.

Let the indices of the black cells be $p_1, p_2, \dots, p_m$ in increasing order. We need to select a set of intervals $[l_j, r_j]$ such that $r_j - l_j + 1 = k$ and for every $i$, there exists some $j$ such that $p_i \in [l_j, r_j]$. We want to minimize the number of intervals.

This looks like a greedy problem. We have a set of points (black cells) on a line. We want to cover them with segments of fixed length $k$.
Consider the leftmost black cell at index $p_1$. To cover this cell, we must choose an interval that includes $p_1$. To maximize the utility of this interval (i.e., to cover as many subsequent black cells as possible), we should extend the interval as far to the right as possible.
So, the optimal interval covering $p_1$ should start at some index $l$ such that $l \le p_1 \le l+k-1$. To maximize the right endpoint $r = l+k-1$, we should choose the smallest possible $l$. However, the interval must cover $p_1$. The constraint is just that the interval covers $p_1$. The interval is defined by its start position. If we pick an interval starting at $p_1$, it covers $[p_1, p_1+k-1]$. If we pick an interval starting at $p_1-k+1$, it covers $[p_1-k+1, p_1]$.
Wait, the problem says "take any $k$ consecutive cells". It doesn't say the interval must start at a black cell. It can start anywhere.
However, if we have a black cell at $p_1$, we *must* cover it. The interval covering $p_1$ will be $[x, x+k-1]$ where $x \le p_1 \le x+k-1$. This implies $p_1 - k + 1 \le x \le p_1$.
To cover the maximum number of black cells to the right of $p_1$, we want the interval to end as far right as possible. The rightmost endpoint is $x+k-1$. Since $x \le p_1$, the maximum right endpoint is $p_1 + k - 1$. This is achieved by choosing the interval starting at $p_1$.
So, the greedy strategy is: find the first uncovered black cell at index $i$. Place an interval starting at $i$ (covering $[i, i+k-1]$). This interval will cover all black cells in this range. Then find the next uncovered black cell and repeat.

